          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a multi-location cleaning supply procurement program?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "One standard for buying and replenishing consumables across several facilities: one approved SKU list for the footprint, a par level and reorder trigger per site, one dispenser standard, one delivery cadence. The supply half sets what you buy; the operating half keeps every site on that standard after launch."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does buying across sites change our price?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The price is the price on the product page, and the pack size you order is what sets the cost per wipe. What consolidation removes is the spend you did not plan: the local retail reorder, the duplicate SKU, and the emergency order that covers a stockout at a 20–30% premium. Discounts are separate from all of it — a subscription, or a discount already running on the product page."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do we consolidate SKUs without breaking how the sites work?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Audit first, then collapse. List every product actually present at each site, identify the duplicates that do the same job, and settle on one approved item per category. Formats can differ by room type, but if the format differs by site for the same room, consolidation fails — the reorder cannot run on two standards at once."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do we set reorder points?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use the par-level formula: (Daily Usage Rate × Lead Time) + Safety Stock. Measure the daily usage rate on a normal week, add a buffer sized to your worst realistic delay, and label the shelf with the resulting number so anyone can trigger the order."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should every location run the same dispenser?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, per room type. Dispenser standardisation is what lets one refill fit every site, which is what lets a single order cover the network. Mixed hardware forces special orders, and special orders are the front door to the emergency premium."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which pack size should the footprint standardise on?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
